{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And the waters were continually receding until the tenth month.</b> The meaning is inverted [i.e., the syntax is reversed], that they were continually receding until the heads of the mountains were seen in the tenth month, which is the month of Tammuz. And Scripture informs us that by day 73 [of their recession] they had diminished fifteen cubits, but the diminution from the beginning until the resting of the ark we do not know how much it was, for Scripture had no need to inform us of the slope [of the water line] nor the diminution. Now, it appears to my mind regarding the ark's drifting that it was because the waters were gushing forth from the deeps and were still boiling, according to the words of our Sages, for because of this it would travel upon the face of the waters, and were it not for this it would have sunk under its own weight, for the many creatures within it and the food and drink were very much indeed. And when the waters subsided from their gushing or from their boiling as well, and also diminished through the wind, immediately the ark entered into the waters due to the heaviness of its load, and it came to rest on the mountain.\n\nAccording to the opinion of the commentators (Ibn Ezra and Radak), the number [of days] at the crest of the waters was one hundred and fifty days, and the resting of the ark and the sighting of the mountain tops forty days thereafter — all this we know by way of prophecy, for Scripture informs us so, but Noah knew nothing except that he sensed the ark had come to rest, and he waited according to his own judgment for a period of time that he thought the waters had subsided. Now, both according to our words and according to the words of our Sages and all the commentators, the mountains of Ararat would be among \"the highest mountains under all the heavens\" (Genesis 7:19), over which the waters were fifteen cubits. This therefore is difficult, for it is well known regarding the Greek mountain that it is much higher than they are, and the land of Ararat is in the lowlands of the circle near Babylon. And perhaps we may say that the diminution on the seventeenth of the seventh month was far greater than fifteen cubits, and before that the heads of the high mountains — not those of Ararat — had appeared, and it chanced that the ark was in the land of Ararat in the seventh month, and it came to rest upon the heads of those mountains. Now, Noah from the time the rain ceased would open the window and close it at his will, and after 73 days from the resting of the ark he looked out the window, and the heads of the mountains of Ararat were visible to him, and he closed it again. And Scripture relates that after forty days he sent the raven, and Scripture did not say \"And it came to pass in such-and-such month and on such-and-such day Noah opened,\" but said \"And it came to pass at the end of forty days,\" to inform us that from the time the mountain heads became visible to Noah he waited forty days, for he thought in his heart that in this time the towers would appear and the trees be uncovered, and the birds would find themselves a resting place wherever they settle, and he opened the window to send the raven. And in the first month, which is the month of Tishrei, the waters dried up, and in the second month, which is the month of Marcheshvan, on the twenty-seventh, the earth dried, and on that day they went out from the ark. Now, the entire tally of the passage is according to its plain sense and meaning.\n\nAnd know that since they agreed that the world was created in Tishrei, as they formulated: \"This day is the beginning of Your works, a remembrance of the first day,\" and so too the order of the seasons is seedtime and harvest, cold and heat, the New Year would be from Tishrei, and the months are counted from it, until we arrived at the Exodus from Egypt, and then the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded to count the months by a different reckoning, as it is stated (Exodus 12:2): \"This month is for you the beginning of months; it is the first for you of the months of the year.\" And from there onward, in all of Scripture, Tishrei is counted as the seventh month. And yet the reckoning of years from it [Tishrei] still remained, as it is written (Exodus 34:22): \"And the Festival of Ingathering at the turn of the year.\" And thus Jonathan ben Uzziel rendered (I Kings 8:2) — \"in the month of Eitanim, which is the seventh month\" — he said: \"in the month of Eitanim, which was formerly called the first month but is now the seventh.\" And in the Mekhilta (Bo 1:8) [on] \"This month is for you\" — the first man [Adam] did not count from it."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "The parenthetical attributions (Ibn Ezra and Radak) appear in the Hebrew text within the lemma passage; I have preserved them as Ramban cites them."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:26:34.214197372Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}